The '''2026''' '''Waltham Forest London Borough Council election''' took place on 7 May 2026.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2026-01-01 |title=Local elections in London will test support for Reform UK and Greens |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c99k0mzj7x8o |access-date=2026-01-19 |website=[[BBC News]] |language=en-GB}}</ref> All 60 members of [[Waltham Forest London Borough Council]] were elected.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Forest |first=Waltham |date=2025-08-20 |title=Labour reveals list of incumbent candidates ahead of 2026 local elections |url=https://walthamforestecho.co.uk/2025/08/20/labour-reveals-list-of-incumbent-candidates-ahead-of-2026-local-elections/ |access-date=2026-01-19 |website=Waltham Forest Echo |language=en-GB}}</ref> The elections took place alongside [[2026 London local elections|local elections in the other London boroughs]] and [[2026 United Kingdom local elections|elections to local authorities across the United Kingdom]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=Number of councils that have requested delay to local elections revealed - is yours one of them? |url=https://news.sky.com/story/local-elections-2026-over-a-third-of-councils-offered-a-delay-have-requested-one-is-yours-on-the-list-13494762 |access-date=2026-01-19 |website=Sky News |language=en}}</ref> The [[Green Party of England and Wales|Green Party]] won control of the council.<ref>{{cite web |title=Waltham Forest election result - Local Elections 2026 |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/election/2026/england/councils/E09000031 |website=BBC News |access-date=8 May 2026}}</ref>

== Background ==
The thirty-two [[London boroughs]] were established in 1965 by the [[London Government Act 1963]]. They are the principal authorities in Greater London and have responsibilities including education, housing, planning, highways, social services, libraries, recreation, waste, environmental health and revenue collection. Some of the powers are shared with the [[Greater London Authority]], which also manages passenger transport, police and fire.<ref>{{Cite web|title=The essential guide to London local government {{!}} London Councils|url=https://www.londoncouncils.gov.uk/who-runs-london/essential-guide-london-local-government|access-date=2021-10-10|website=www.londoncouncils.gov.uk}}</ref>

Since its formation, Waltham Forest has generally been under [[Labour Party (UK)|Labour]] control or [[no overall control]] with one period of [[Conservative Party (UK)|Conservative]] control from 1968 to 1971. Labour won an overall majority from no overall control in the [[2010 Waltham Forest London Borough Council election|2010 election]], with 36 seats and 38.9% of the vote across the borough; with the Conservatives winning eighteen seats and the [[Liberal Democrats (UK)|Liberal Democrats]] winning the remaining six. The Liberal Democrats lost all their seats in the [[2014 Waltham Forest London Borough Council election|2014 election]], with Labour winning 44 and the Conservatives winning 16. In [[2018 Waltham Forest London Borough Council election|2018]], Labour increased its majority with 46 seats to the Conservatives' 14. In the [[2022 Waltham Forest London Borough Council election|most recent election in 2022]], Labour again extended its majority by winning 47 seats with 59.2% of the vote across the borough while the Conservatives won 13 seats with 22.2% of the vote. The [[Green Party of England and Wales|Green Party]] received 7.5% of the vote and the Liberal Democrats received 7.4% of the vote but neither won any seats.

